1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
A fresh walkthrough of the current app, using a separate copy of your existing students, faculty and attendance data.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
The Admin signs in with a username and password. Password characters stay hidden.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
A six-digit authenticator code completes the second login step.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:34,000
The current overview shows 4 students, 3 faculty, 4 sections and 10 attendance sessions.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:43,000
The original students are retained: Anjanaa, Pradhyut, Suhas and Harsha K, with their sections and enrolled subjects.

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:51,000
The existing faculty are Shravya, Bhargavi and Praffulata. The Admin manages their profiles and account status.

7
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The timetable connects a subject, section, faculty member, day and period. Dashboards use it to show upcoming classes.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,000
The current plan contains 10 classes per subject. This lets the system calculate how many remaining classes a student must attend.

9
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:22,000
At the 75% threshold, Anjanaa has 2/5 (40%) and Harsha has 3/5 (60%). Both appear on the shortage list.

10
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The Admin can create an encrypted backup. A saved backup can later be used for recovery.

11
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
A new backup is created successfully in the demo copy. It is listed as Complete with a download option.

12
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Shravya signs in as F001. Her dashboard shows her assigned class, 2 students, upcoming periods and one attendance shortage.

13
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Select Software Engineering, 15 November and Period 1. Load the roster for the assigned class.

14
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:05,000
In the recording copy, mark Pradhyut Present and Harsha Absent. Every student needs a status before saving.

15
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:13,000
The saved session displays the recorded marks. Any later change goes through the separate correction workflow.

16
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:22,000
Change Harsha from Absent to Present and enter a reason. The page also shows the permitted correction deadline.

17
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:33,000
The history records F001, the time, the old and new status, and the reason. The correction is now traceable.

18
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:41,000
Choose the date range and report scope. Here the report includes the faculty member’s visible classes on 15 November.

19
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:50,000
After the correction, the report shows 2 Present and 0 Absent. CSV and PDF export options are available.

20
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:58,000
Both students have 1/1 attendance for the selected day. This filtered percentage is different from their overall attendance.

21
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:09,000
Harsha sees the updated demo total: 4/6 (66.67%). He must attend all 4 remaining planned classes to finish above 75%.

22
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Students can review their own attendance, filter by subject or date, and see the remaining classes they need to attend.

23
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:27,000
The selected subject lists each date, period and attendance mark. The demonstration date now shows Present.

24
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Live totals appear above the alerts. The earlier alert retains its original 60% figure; the current demo total is 66.67%.

25
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Logging out returns the student to the login page. This walkthrough used the current code; your working database was left unchanged.
